## New slide batch — 2026-10-26

Compared the current Slides directory with the recorded source mapping and full-file hashes. Six decks were absent from the mapping and had no lecture notes: lectures 8, 10, 11, 12, 13 and 14, totaling 227 new PDF pages. All eight earlier source PDFs match the 2026-10-12 baseline, so no substantive PDF revision is inferred for those decks.

Created one substantive English note per new deck, with source page references, definitions and assumptions, algorithms, correctness arguments, derivations, worked traces, takeaways, revision answers, and 24 original exam-style exercises with collapsed solutions. Added 18 local slide-page images with captions. No external visual was needed: the supplied diagrams cover the new explanations. Animation sequences are consolidated, and covers/acknowledgments are recorded as provenance rather than transcribed.

| Note | Significant explanations and qualifications |
|---|---|
| [[Selection and Order Statistics]] | Exact paired-extrema counts; local rank updates; conditional linear-expectation proof with odd/even sums; full-group median-of-medians discard proof; unequal recurrence; why the simple triples proof lacks linear slack. Page 7's strict “less than” claim fails for odd sizes. Rendered pp. 22–23 and 35 have correct outer parentheses. |
| [[Primality Testing and Cryptosystems]] | Numerical versus bit-length complexity; pseudoprime/Carmichael distinction; checked-power trace for 341; explicit standard Miller–Rabin variant and independent error budget; output-conditioned probability correction; RSA gcd condition, full-domain correctness, extended-Euclid and encryption/signature examples. Page 15's base-count domain is contradicted by n=9; page 24's nondivisibility condition is insufficient. |
| [[Randomized Data Structures]] | Unique min-priority treaps; indicator/harmonic path proof; rotations and full update traces; exact expected rotation formula; split/join and discrete-key qualification; skip-list predecessor trace, promotion/space/height calculations, and distinction between height and search-path bounds. Page 24's “2 rotations” is an upper-bound shorthand, and its “depth” counts visited nodes. |
| [[Dynamic Programming and LCS]] | Both recurrence proofs; base cases; state counts versus recursive occurrences; full small and slide tables; explicit witness traceback; memoized worst-case versus visited-state work; rolling-row length computation. Page 9's masks select positions, not necessarily distinct symbol strings. Linear-space witness reconstruction is distinguished from length-only storage. |
| [[Binary Decision Diagrams]] | Shannon cofactors; reduction/canonicity proof; unique versus computed tables; full ITE trace; operand-state complexity with cache-retention assumptions; complement normalization; constant checking/composition; ordering and collection; ZDD semantics and declared universe; final combinational-set diagrams. Page 6's output-linear wording is not a general bound; page 31's final locality bullet is incomplete. |
| [[Amortized Analysis]] | Exact total-copy formula; accounting invariant; telescoping potential; full ten-operation trace; first-operation exception; allocation-per-slot alternative prompted by the bank's wording. The first-insertion issue is explicitly posed by page 42, not presented as an unnoticed error. |

Integrated all six notes into the index, supplied-lecture reading order, topic routes and practice links. Older lecture changes are focused: introduction coverage/status, sorting's next lecture and selection connection, divide-and-conquer applications and modular powering, randomized-algorithm applications, and PRAM notebook availability. Existing filenames, frontmatter, annotations and images are retained.

The question bank changed during this review to its current 552 unanswered entries from 58 distinct 2022–2026 papers. Preserve that newer content: this slide-note pass only replaces seven obsolete “no dedicated note” notices with relevant study links. It does not write answers, change question IDs, or claim to have performed the bank expansion. The index now reflects the current count.

External explanations were checked against CMU's selection, LCS and amortized-analysis lectures; Menezes–van Oorschot–Vanstone's *Handbook of Applied Cryptography*, Chapters 4 and 8; Aragon–Seidel's treap paper; Pugh's UMD-hosted *Skip List Cookbook*; Bryant's annotated BDD paper; and CUDD's user/programmer manuals. Citations are placed next to the relevant clarifications. The direct UMD original skip-list PDF and an attempted Rice-hosted BDD reference were inaccessible; the UMD Cookbook, Bryant paper and CUDD manuals were available alternatives. No later WeBeep announcement was accessed.

## Current gaps and scope — 2026-10-26

- All fourteen supplied slide decks have a substantive corresponding note. No revised baseline PDF was detected. The collection is not a claim of complete exam coverage.
- No lecture-9 deck is present. Further material remains needed for approximation, competitive/online algorithms including move-to-front, further string-matching topics, disjoint sets, and broader parallel tools/patterns. Historical bank questions are evidence of past questions, not new current-year syllabus rules. The introduction explicitly excludes CUDA from the current syllabus; keep its historical bank entries without treating them as current requirements.
- Lecture 12's O(min(m,n))-space exercise is explained for the LCS length. A complete linear-space witness-reconstruction algorithm is not supplied in that deck. Lecture 11 gives treap Union but no skip-list Union algorithm; the corresponding historical question is not fully covered by the supplied slides. The bank remains intentionally unanswered.
- Five local notebooks now exist in Notebooks/: [[lecture3-DandC-profiling.ipynb]], [[01_pram_reduction_prefix_sum.ipynb]], [[02_amdahl_gustafson_scaling.ipynb]], [[03_strong_weak_scaling.ipynb]], and [[04_ispc.ipynb]]. Their titles/section headings were inspected, resolving the earlier “no local notebooks” status. Their code, benchmark outputs and external downloads were not executed or independently validated in this slide-note pass; equivalence to original Colab links remains unestablished.
- Source years differ: selection/primality covers are 2026–2027; randomized-data-structure and BDD covers 2025–2026; LCS 2021–2022; amortized analysis 2023–2024. Retain these labels alongside the earlier provenance distinctions. Assessment rules remain attributed to the uploaded introduction, without verification against later announcements.
- BDD page 31 ends with an incomplete memory-locality bullet. Its missing continuation is not reconstructed. No other new slide page was unreadable; mathematical overbars, roots, superscripts, and grouping were checked on renders when extraction was ambiguous.

## Verification — 2026-10-26

Validated 18 Markdown files (17 course files and Study Note Prompts): 1915 actual internal references resolved, including 741 heading/block targets and 824 physical-PDF-page references. All 78 image embeds loaded successfully (73 distinct images). YAML properties, table columns and code-fence balance passed. Literal links inside fenced prompt examples are excluded from reference resolution. All fourteen slide hashes match the recorded mapping; the earlier eight remain unchanged. The 552 current question entries are byte-for-byte unchanged from the snapshot taken immediately before the seven study-link edits.

Recalculated the new selection trace and odd/even expected-size sums; checked deterministic discard thresholds; compared checked-power and standard strong tests on small odd integers; verified the pseudoprime/root examples and RSA recovery for every representative in both illustrative moduli; enumerated priority permutations through size seven for treap path expectations and actual insertion rotations; recalculated both small LCS tables and every cell of the slide example; checked ITE truth values/complement identities and the ordering example; and checked both dynamic-table cost models and potential identities through 10,000 appends. No discrepancy remained. These finite checks support the supplied derivations and are not substitutes for their proofs. No notebook or benchmark execution is claimed.

Intermediate source text, selected renders, source manifest, arithmetic/reference audit scripts and results, and pre-edit snapshots are in outputs/aapp-update-2026-10-26/. Snapshot filenames use a non-Markdown extension so they do not create duplicate vault-note titles.
